\chapter{Root and directory-local manifests}
\label{ch:62}
\index{Root and directory-local manifests}

The root \OnxCodeTerm{On1xfile} is a UTF-8, line-oriented manifest. It declares project policy and target inputs through its participating directories. Directory-local \OnxCodeTerm{On1xfile.m} and \OnxCodeTerm{On1xfile.i} files declare source/library and interface information. The manifest parser owns the grammar, and the regular source loader owns import and type semantics. The existence of Lua-based profile logic does not make the root manifest an arbitrary Lua program.

\section{Make an undeclared input reproducible}
Start with two functions in separate files and add only one file to the directory manifest. Build and compare the dependency report with the filesystem. Add the missing declaration, then confirm that the build report records the selected canonical paths. Pair a declared interface with the matching source stem to reproduce the signature checks in \cref{ch:34}. Preserve manifest bytes and compiler options when investigating a plan difference. Two projects can share the same physical source files yet select different targets or compiler policies.

\section{Resolve the three names of a helper}
Consider a library named \OnxCodeTerm{toolkit} whose root manifest says
\OnxCodeTerm{directory "lib"}. Put \OnxCodeTerm{convert.on1x} in that
directory. Its local module manifest contains these two lines:
\begin{OnxCodeBlock}[numbers=none,language={}]
on1xfile 1
library "convert.on1x"
\end{OnxCodeBlock}
The local spelling is relative to the manifest; the filesystem spelling
from the root is \OnxCodeTerm{lib/convert.on1x}; the qualified name in
a dependent project is \OnxCodeTerm{toolkit.convert}. The directory used
to discover files does not become an extra component of that module name.
After declaring the dependency, a client can write
\OnxCodeTerm{import toolkit.convert as C;}. Changing only the alias
changes the client's vocabulary, not the package that supplied the file.

Now give the module a public signature. Put the interface beside the
implementation and add \OnxCodeTerm{interface "convert.on1xi"} after the
version line in the local interface manifest. Predict the result of moving
only the interface to a second declared directory: even if its filename
and signature text are identical, it no longer has the required local
implementation partner. Putting it back resolves the pairing; changing
its return type can still fail the compiler's signature check. These are
two different reasons for a library build to reject an interface, even
when no target calls the function. Finally, compare the published interface
bytes to the declared input: clients receive the checked source package,
not a binary module inferred from the alias. Renaming the physical module
also requires updating its manifest role and the client's import; moving
only the directory root can preserve the qualified name.

